    "source_selection_rationale": "Diagnosis and plan: For handwritten digit classification with the given constraints, MNIST training splits provide ample per-class coverage and are the canonical source for learning digits in grayscale. Allowed_sources_by_class map every digit to mnist_cls_train (and mnist_cls_test for evaluation). Since the target_images_per_class is 1500, we select 1500 training samples per class from mnist_cls_train as the primary pool. This yields a balanced, domain-consistent, and compact dataset suitable for CPU inference on a 4 GB RAM machine when paired with a lightweight model like MobileNetV2. We do not include mnist_cls_test in the training pool per the official-splits guidance. Per-class pool size: 1500 images for digits 0\u20139, total primary pool 15,000 images. Approximate total download size (raw digits): about 15,000 images \u00d7 28 \u00d7 28 \u00d7 1 byte \u2248 12 MB for image data, plus labels and overhead, typically under 15\u201325 MB in practice. This size comfortably fits the 500 MB runtime memory budget and aligns with the requirement to process images efficiently on CPU. The default download budget does not appear to constrain this choice since 15k MNIST train samples are well within typical provisioning expectations. If higher diversity or robustness were needed, one could consider careful domain-generalization augmentation or a small supplementary source, but given the strict per-class, domain-consistent constraint and the performance target, sticking to the mnist_train pool provides a clean, reliable baseline.",
